  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Michael King made the game smaller than it was. Six scoreless innings against the Yankees. A shutout line that let the Padres stay ahead wire-to-wire without ever needing to break sweat, and without needing much from the bats either.",
    "what_this_means": "This is what fragile wins look like when the pitching is locked. The starter consumed six full innings and left zero damage, which meant the bullpen only had to survive. Rodriguez struck out two in the first inning of work. Morejon leaked two runs in the eighth but it didn't matter because the lead was already two, not one. Mason Miller closed it with three strikeouts. The formula here is not complicated: shut them down early, hold it late, finish before the ninth even starts. But it only works if King keeps delivering like this. The problem is that one-run wins have a shelf life, and the Padres are stacking them now. Four runs to beat the Yankees, and that is not a margin that survives careless mistakes. They left three runners on base in the second inning, the critical moment, with nobody scoring from it. That inning told you everything: runners available, bats unable to cash. Campusano and Machado each hit one to bail them out, but broad production is fragile when only two guys convert. Three straight one-run wins. That math gets harder.",
    "what_to_watch": "Washington arrives tonight with Jake Irvin on the mound, carrying a 5.57 ERA into a Padres lineup still waiting for a third hitter to show up consistently. Nick Pivetta takes the ball for San Diego.",
